1. the factor and its cost identity
  ok   stop width is a percentage of entry
  ok   and it is unsigned — a short is the same width
  ok   a zero entry has no width rather than an infinite one
  ok   cost in R is the venue round trip over the stop width — the hg-v919 identity
  ok   so a wider stop costs LESS in R, which is why tight stops are the cohort under test

2. expectancy is per unit of RISK DEPLOYED, not per trade
  ok   flat weighting is the plain mean
  ok   and halving EVERY weight changes nothing — it is a ratio
  ok   expectancy divides by risk deployed (0.6), not by trade count (0.375)
  ok   and the risk figure is the sum of the weights that were actually put up
  ok   trading one of two deploys half the risk, and the figure records it
  ok   a scheme that trades nothing deploys nothing
  ok   and has no expectancy rather than a flattering zero

3. the schemes are what they claim to be
  ok   flat is the baseline, 1 everywhere
  ok   a cliff is a reject, and it is inclusive at the bar
  ok   the same at 0.50
  ok   a ramp tops out at full size
  ok   and floors at a quarter rather than at zero — that is what makes it NOT a reject
  ok   it is monotone in stop width
  ok   half-size is the crude version of the same idea
  ok   flat stays a weight in [0,1] across the range
  ok   cliff028 stays a weight in [0,1] across the range
  ok   cliff050 stays a weight in [0,1] across the range
  ok   ramp050 stays a weight in [0,1] across the range
  ok   ramp028 stays a weight in [0,1] across the range
  ok   halfTight stays a weight in [0,1] across the range
  ok   sqrt050 stays a weight in [0,1] across the range

4. the fill bound moves the rows it should, and only those
  ok   unfilled rows are not trades and are dropped at both ends
  ok   the both-touch row survives
  ok   at the lower bound both-touch is a loss, like the plain loss beside it
  ok   at the upper bound it is credited as a win
  ok   and the ordinary win is untouched by the bound — only the ambiguous row moves
  ok   net is always below gross: the cost is charged, never rebated

5. the verdict on the real books — and it is a REFUSAL
  ok   flat judged against itself wins 0 of 16 — a tie is not an improvement
  ok   so the baseline can never be reported as holding
  ok   both desks are measured
  ok   on 2445 and 8132 settled trades
  ok   GOLD SCALP: NOTHING is unanimous over 4 disjoint windows x 2 bounds x gross+net — no sizing rule ships
  ok     each scheme faced all 16 tests
  ok   OMNIGOLD: NOTHING is unanimous over 4 disjoint windows x 2 bounds x gross+net — no sizing rule ships
  ok     each scheme faced all 16 tests
  ok   on GOLD SCALP the best GRADED scheme (10/16) does not beat the best cliff (14/16) — sizing is not better than rejecting, which was the hypothesis
  ok   ramp050 DOES improve the pooled whole-book net (-0.1573 -> -0.0908)
  ok     and still fails, at 10/16 — the hg-v920 trap, caught rather than shipped
  ok   cliff050 posts the best score on GOLD SCALP while keeping only 9% of the risk — it is barely trading
  ok   and no scheme deploys more risk than flat, which none can

6. the refusal is recorded where the next reader will look
  ok   the script states the gap it was written to close
  ok   and the exact quantity it measures
  ok   and the bar a scheme has to clear
  ok   and that anything short of it is not acted on
  ok   the refusal is in AGENTS.md so it is not silently re-tried

